            <ArticleTitle>Cryptogenic cerebral embolism in women taking oral contraceptives.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>507-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fourteen women taking oral contraceptives were admitted during a five-year period because of acute cerebrovascular lesions. A diagnosis of major cerebral embolism was established in four of them. No source of embolism was found, and thorough investigation failed to reveal any predisposing illness. Cerebral embolism was a probable diagnosis in several of the remaining 10 patients. A comparison was made with the strokes occurring in women not taking contraceptive pills in corresponding age groups.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
